WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.300
What lasts: fundamentals, a learning loop and change you did not choose.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:09.680
Vina spent her first year mastering the team's reporting tool.

3
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:17.700
On Monday morning the team hears that from March, a new AI tool will replace it.

4
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:25.090
Her first thought: a year wasted. Her second: they are replacing us. Neither is a fact yet.

5
00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:31.750
Look at what she really learned that year. Above ground are the tools. Below ground are the fundamentals. Tools are replaced every few years. The fundamentals carry over, and they compound.

6
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:41.020
Logic, statistics, clear writing, and judgement about her field. So for anything you learn, ask: which part of this survives when the tool is replaced?

7
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:49.950
To keep the fundamentals growing, she sets up a small loop. One source she trusts. One small project. One log, a line each week. And one defended hour a week.

8
00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:58.370
A skill nobody can see is invisible to those who could reward it. So Vina lists hers, with the public proof beside each one. Presenting: nothing yet. That gap is her next project.

9
00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:06.840
Back to Monday. Before she reacts, Vina pauses and separates fact from interpretation. The fact is one sentence: the team will use the new tool from March. Then she chooses one action inside her control: she asks to join the pilot.

10
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:12.830
A senior colleague says the last new system failed too. Vina does not argue. She asks: what went wrong that time? People accept a change more readily when they have had a voice in it.

11
00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:20.230
Tools expire. Fundamentals compound. One logged hour a week beats a panic sprint.

12
00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:25.030
Now it is your turn. Name one fundamental in your field, and one place where you will show it publicly.
